After the red trains shouldered through countries whose names he loved to mispronounce, after the ferries lifted and fell on the iron breath of northern seas, after passports softened in his pockets and maps tore along their brightest folds;

after cedar rain, salt marsh, desert noon, after the green cathedral of a forest where every leaf held one fluent syllable of light, after mountains arrived at dawn like blue animals and vanished by evening into weather;

after stadiums, cinder tracks, the leather percussion of balls against walls, after his body had been a quick republic of muscle and air, after the whistle, the starting gun, the clean astonishment of clearing the bar;

after the winter stars drilled their cold distances into him, after Orion leaned above the roof, after Mars burned rust-red and the moon carried its pale cargo over the sleeping town;

after the car rides, then the porch, then the garden path measured by a cane;

after the garden became a window, after the window became a square of sky, after the square of sky became a reflection in the dark television;

after the corridor with its polished horizon, after the elevator’s brief descent, after the wheels beneath him replaced every road;

after his world contracted to the hospital room, to the bed, to the raised rail, to the call button glowing like a last domesticated star;

after the nurses’ shoes whispered in and out, after the machines translated his heart into green peaks, after language loosened and names drifted beyond reach;

after breath became the only journey—outward, inward, outward—each voyage shorter than the last;

after even pain withdrew to a small black country behind his ribs;

there remained the hand in his hand, though he could no longer tell whose, and a thread of morning entering beneath the blind.

He watched the thread brighten.

Perhaps it was a road. Perhaps a river. Perhaps the white line of a field under floodlights, or the tail of a comet crossing distances no body could cross.

His mouth remembered before he did.

And he smiled into death.
